<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montgomery, Alabama</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montgomery is the capital of the US state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>, on the Alabama River. In February 1861 delegates of seceding states met there to form the Confederate States of America, and it was the Confederate capital until May 1861. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a> of 1955–1956, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, was a turning point of the civil rights movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montgomery-alabama.jpg" alt="Montgomery, Alabama" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carol M. Highsmith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_Montgomery,_Alabama_LCCN2011646683.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Montgomery stands beside the Alabama River on the Gulf Coastal Plain, on rolling terrain about 220 feet above sea level. Downtown lies along the southern bank of the river, about 6 miles downstream from the confluence of the Coosa and Tallapoosa rivers. The city was named for Richard Montgomery, a major-general of the Continental Army. The Alabama State Capitol stands on a hill at one end of Dexter Avenue, on which also lies the Dexter Avenue Baptist Church, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a> was pastor. Both are National Historic Landmarks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before European colonisation the east bank of the Alabama River was inhabited by the Alibamu, who with the Coushatta on the west side were descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture</a>. Both spoke closely related Muskogean languages. The present city stands on the site of two Alibamu towns: Ikanatchati, meaning red earth, and Towassa, built on a bluff. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto</a> and his expedition passed through Ikanatchati in 1540 and camped for a week at Towassa.</p><p>Montgomery was incorporated in 1819 as a merger of two towns on the river. In 1846 it replaced Tuscaloosa as the state capital, a change that reflected the shift of power to south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> grew in the Black Belt and Mobile rose as a port. In 1840 Montgomery County led the state in cotton production, and by 1911 the city processed 160,000 to 200,000 bales a year. In February 1861 Montgomery was chosen the first capital of the Confederate States of America, until the seat of government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richmond-virginia">Richmond, Virginia</a>, in May. In the middle of the 20th century it was a major centre of the civil rights movement, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montgomery-bus-boycott">Montgomery bus boycott</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selma-to-montgomery-marches">Selma to Montgomery marches</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Montgomery is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and the county seat of Montgomery County, with a population of 200,603 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The governor's office, the Alabama Legislature and the Alabama Supreme Court are located there. Maxwell Air Force Base gives it a large military presence. Its civil rights history is commemorated at the Legacy Museum, the Civil Rights Memorial and the National Memorial for Peace and Justice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Montgomery, Alabama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their conventions sent delegates to <b>Montgomery, Alabama</b>, on 4 February 1861.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 1 December 1955 Rosa Parks was arrested in <b>Montgomery, Alabama</b>, for refusing to give up her bus seat to a white passenger.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Rosa Parks was arrested in <b>Montgomery, Alabama</b>, for refusing to give her seat to a white passenger.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-1">1 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Delegates from six seceded states opened a convention in <b>Montgomery, Alabama</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-4">4 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
